In article <774559187.MC1803@mclink.it>, Adam Atkinson <MC1803@mclink.it> writes:

>(i)
>I read an unknown scroll, and it erupted in a tower of flame. An unknown
>potion
>I was carrying boiled. The game then asked me what I wanted to call cloudy
>potions rather than asking me what I wanted to call scrolls called 
>"foobar" (or whatever it was). Or was I supposed to have learned something
>about the potion from the way it boiled? (Maybe the game decided for me
>that I knew what kind the scroll was)

It's a feature. The scroll will be named by the game for you - as it is obvious
that it was a scroll of fire. From the boiling potion you got so called 
vapour effect - same you get if you break a potion bottle near to you,
or if someone throws a bottle at you. Generally the vapour effect is similar
to normal effect, only much weaker and you are supposed to learn something
from it. That's why the game asked you for name for the potion...
